  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Remember that we have seen the series
  <md>
  \arctan(x) = x - \frac13 x^3 + \frac 15 x^5 + \ldots +
  (-1)^n\frac1{2n+1} x^{2n+1} + \ldots.
  </md></p>
        <p>Write the series to find <m>\arctan(0.8)</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now set up this up in a Google sheet like this:</p>
        <p>\begin{tabular}{c|c|c|c}
      <m>n</m> &amp; <m>x</m> &amp; <m>n^{th}</m> term &amp; Sum of <m>n</m> terms 
      \hline
      0&amp;	0.8&amp;	0.8 &amp;	0.8  
      1&amp;	0.8&amp;	-0.170667 &amp;	0.629333 
      2&amp;	0.8&amp;	0.065536 &amp;	0.694869 
      3&amp;	0.8&amp;	-0.029959 &amp;	0.664910 
      4&amp;	0.8&amp;	0.014913 &amp;	0.679823 
    \end{tabular}</p>
        <p>Estimate <m>\arctan(0.8)</m> with an error less than <m>10^{-6}</m> using the
  alternating series test to determine how many terms you need to do
  this.  What is your estimate and how many terms do you need to
  guarantee this accuracy?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Modify your spreadsheet to estimate <m>\arctan(0.9)</m> with an error
  less than <m>10^{-6}</m> using the 
  alternating series test to determine how many terms you need to do
  this.  What is your estimate and how many terms do you need to
  guarantee this accuracy?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="3in">
      <statement>
        <p>The function
  <md>
  C(x) = \int_0^x \cos(t^2)~dt
  </md>
  is called the Fresnel integral function and is used in optics.
  Maybe you've visited a lighthouse in Michigan and seen a ``Fresnel''
  lens.  This is not an integral that we can evaluate by
  antidifferentiating.</p>
        <p>Start with the Taylor series for the cosine function and use it to
  write a Taylor series for <m>C(x)</m>.  Be sure to write the general
  <m>n^{th}</m> term.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now set up a Google sheet to estimate <m>C(1)</m> with an error less than
  <m>10^{-6}</m>.  Use the alternating series test to guarantee this
  accuracy.  How many terms do you need?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="3in">
      <statement>
        <p>The function
  <md>
  E(x) = \int_0^x e^{-t^2}~dt
  </md>
  is important in statistics as it relates to the ``normal
  distribution'' or bell curve.  Once again, we can't evaluate this
  integral by antidifferentiating.</p>
        <p>Start with the Taylor series for the cosine function and use it to
  write a Taylor series for <m>e^x</m>.  Be sure to write the general
  <m>n^{th}</m> term.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now set up a Google sheet to estimate <m>E(1)</m> with an error less than
  <m>10^{-6}</m>.  Use the alternating series test to guarantee this
  accuracy.  What is your estimate and how many terms do you need?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Modify your Google sheet to estimate <m>E(2)</m> with an error less than
  <m>10^{-6}</m>.  Use the alternating series test to guarantee this
  accuracy.  What is your estimate and how many terms do you need?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
